# BoxLang Java interop

Prefer idiomatic BoxLang Java access over legacy `createObject( "java", ... )`.

## Preferred forms

```boxlang
import java:java.io.File
import java:java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap
import java:java.lang.System

var root = new File( projectPath ).getCanonicalFile()
var map = new ConcurrentHashMap()
var nowMs = System.currentTimeMillis()
```

Inline `new java:fully.qualified.Name( ... )` is fine for one-off uses.
`createObject( "java", "..." ).init( ... )` still works but is verbose and legacy-style.

## Import / variable name collisions (case-insensitive)

BoxLang identifiers are **case-insensitive**. An `import` brings the class short name into the same namespace as local variables, properties, and arguments.

**Do not** reuse the imported class name as a variable (any casing):

```boxlang
import java:java.io.File

// BAD — `file` / `File` / `FILE` all collide with the imported class
var file = new File( path )

// GOOD — use a distinct name
var candidate = new File( path )
var rootFile = new File( path )
var canonical = new File( path ).getCanonicalFile()
```

Same rule for other short imports, for example:

| Import | Avoid as variable | Prefer |
|--------|-------------------|--------|
| `java.io.File` | `file`, `File` | `candidate`, `rootFile`, `listingFile` |
| `java.lang.System` | `system`, `System` | (call `System.*` statics; don't shadow) |
| `java.util.List` | `list`, `List` | `items`, `rows`, `entries` |
| `java.net.URI` | `uri`, `Uri` | `endpoint`, `baseUri` |

If a short name would force awkward locals, skip the import and use the prefixed form:

```boxlang
var file = new java:java.io.File( path ).getCanonicalFile()
```

Or alias the import when the docs/runtime support it:

```boxlang
import java:java.io.File as JFile
var file = new JFile( path )
```
